# Project Rules

## Project Shape
- This project is configured for SwarmForge with two Codex-backed agents:
  coder and cleaner.
- The coder is the master worktree role.
- Project language: Kotlin Multiplatform, targeting Android and iOS. A small
  part of the code is Swift in the iOS source sets.
- Build and test through the Gradle wrapper `./gradlew` in the assigned
  worktree. Never `gradle` from `$PATH`, and never Maven.

## Local Configuration
- Preserve project-local SwarmForge configuration under `swarmforge/`.
- Keep swarm state local under `.swarmforge/`, worktrees under `.worktrees/`,
  and shared scripts under `swarmforge/scripts/`.

## Workflow
- The cleaner receives coder handoffs in batch mode.
- Do not create or use Gherkin, IR, acceptance tests, Gherkin mutation, or
  property tests.
- Use unit tests and language-local verification only. `aps-kotlin`,
  `gherkin-parser`, `ir-dry-checker`, and `gherkin-mutator` are not part of this
  pack; do not install or run them.
- Unit tests are the only behavioral evidence this pack produces, and in a
  Multiplatform project that makes the source set they live in a decision.
  Default to `commonTest` so a test runs on every target. Put a test in
  `androidUnitTest` or `iosTest` only for behavior that is genuinely
  platform-specific.

## Handoffs
- Prefer terse, explicit handoffs that report state and request
  role-appropriate review.
- Do not include sender process narrative.

## Cleaner Handoffs
- When a helper-delivered task is from cleaner, `ready_for_next.sh` has
  already merged that commit. Run unit tests and fix any failures. Then run
  `done_with_current.sh`. Do not send a `git_handoff` downstream. Do not
  invent `git merge`. Apart from verification, do not apply other
  role-specific work to that cleaner handoff.

## Ownership
- Do not change another role's prompt or workflow ownership without explicit
  user direction.
